<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Death</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1346">1346</a>–<a href="/years/1353">1353</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Death was the pandemic of plague that swept across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the mid-fourteenth century, killing a large share of the population. It spread along the trade routes linked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a>. The epidemic weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, damaged the economy and governance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and is also cited in the histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-death.jpg" alt="Black Death" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flappiefh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1346-1353_spread_of_the_Black_Death_in_Europe_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…commerce that the khanate taxed during its period of effective control formed part of the transcontinental exchange of goods, technologies and ideas characteristic of the Pax Mongolica era — and of the routes along which, it is widely believed, the plague that became the <b>Black Death</b> spread westward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Black Death</b> of 1348 hit Catalonia especially hard, and in the fifteenth century Valencia overtook Barcelona as the largest and richest city of the Crown, building its Silk Exchange, the Llotja de la Seda, in 1482–1498.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Egypt it sustained the Italian colonies of the Crimea, and the siege of Caffa in 1346, during an epidemic in the besieging army, is often linked with the arrival of the <b>Black Death</b> in Europe through Genoese ships, though the exact routes of the plague remain debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Black Death</b> further weakened the region, and by the mid-fourteenth century effective Mongol political authority in Persia had disappeared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The population grew to perhaps five million or more by 1300 before famine and the <b>Black Death</b> of 1348–1349, which killed perhaps a third to a half of the people, cut it drastically; labour shortages loosened serfdom and helped provoke the Peasants’ Revolt of 1381.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Black Death</b> from 1348 and the wars that followed cut the population sharply before a long recovery after 1450.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
